{"summary":"Annales Veteris Testamenti, a Prima Mundi Origine Deducti ('Annals of the Old Testament, Deduced from the First Origins of the World') is a 1650 work by Archbishop James Ussher of Armagh, representing the most comprehensive biblical chronology ever attempted. Drawing on the Hebrew Bible, Septuagint, ancient Near Eastern sources, Josephus, and Scaligerian chronological methodology, Ussher calculated that Creation occurred at nightfall preceding Sunday, 23 October 4004 BCE — in the Julian calendar. The work covered every major Old Testament event from Creation through the death of Nebuchadnezzar, correlating each with a precise astronomical and civil date.

The Annales was not a fundamentalist polemic but a serious work of humanist scholarship. Ussher cross-referenced Ptolemy's canon of kings, Eusebius's Chronicle, the Syriac Peshitta, and Arabic astronomical tables alongside the Hebrew text. His methodology was rigorous by seventeenth-century standards — he made explicit astronomical calculations to identify the Julian dates corresponding to biblical events, and carefully weighted different manuscript traditions.

The work's extraordinary cultural impact came in 1701, when Bishop William Lloyd inserted Ussher's dates in the margins of a new edition of the Authorised King James Bible. This edition was reprinted for over 250 years, making 4004 BCE the de facto Protestant creation date. Scientists from Hutton to Darwin explicitly framed their deep-time discoveries as refutations of Ussher's timeline, making the Annales an unintended catalyst of the geological and biological revolutions.","causes":["The Protestant Reformation's emphasis on *sola scriptura* spurred a demand for precise, verifiable biblical chronologies.","The 17th-century intellectual environment valued systematic compilation and encyclopedic knowledge, encouraging comprehensive historical projects.","Archbishop James Ussher's profound linguistic and theological scholarship provided the necessary expertise for such an ambitious undertaking."],"effects":["It established a widely accepted biblical chronology that fundamentally influenced historical dating in the Western world for centuries.","It became a foundational text for literal biblical interpretation and young-earth creationist thought.","It eventually sparked significant scientific and theological debates as geological and evolutionary evidence challenged its literal timeline."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"james-ussher","sourceName":"James Ussher","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testamenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"Archbishop James Ussher authored the comprehensive biblical chronology *Annales Veteris Testamenti* in 1650."},{"sourceSlug":"protestant-reformation","sourceName":"Protestant Reformation","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testamenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"The Reformation's emphasis on scriptural authority fostered an intellectual climate demanding precise biblical chronologies like Ussher's *Annales*."},{"sourceSlug":"annales-veteris-testamenti","sourceName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"dublin-ireland","targetName":"Dublin, Ireland","context":"The *Annales Veteris Testamenti* was published in Dublin in 1650, reflecting Ussher's prominent position in Irish intellectual life."},{"sourceSlug":"annales-veteris-testamenti","sourceName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"isaac-newton","targetName":"Isaac Newton","context":"Isaac Newton, a contemporary scholar, was influenced by Ussher's meticulous approach to biblical chronology, though he developed his own distinct calculations."},{"sourceSlug":"charles-darwin","sourceName":"Charles Darwin","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testamenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"Charles Darwin's 1859 theory of evolution by natural selection fundamentally challenged the literal biblical timeline established by Ussher's *Annales*."}],"texts":[{"slug":"annalium-pars-posterior","name":"Annalium Pars Posterior","year":"1654","role":"Ussher's sequel continuing the timeline from Solomon to the New Testament"},{"slug":"king-james-bible","name":"King James Bible (1701 edition)","year":"1701","role":"The 1701 edition which first printed Ussher's dates in the margins"}],"evidence":[{"tier":"A","citation":"Ussher, James. Annales Veteris Testamenti. London: Flesher & Robinson, 1650. The complete primary text."},{"tier":"B","citation":"Barr, James. 'Why the World Was Created in 4004 BC.' Bulletin of the John Rylands Library 67 (1985): 575–608."},{"tier":"B","citation":"Gould, Stephen Jay. 'Fall in the House of Ussher.' Natural History, November 1991."}],"timeline":[{"year":"1650","event":"Published in London by Flesher and Robinson; immediately recognised as the definitive biblical chronology"},{"year":"1654","event":"Sequel Annalium Pars Posterior published, completing the timeline through the New Testament"},{"year":"1701","event":"Bishop William Lloyd inserts Ussher's dates in the margins of the Authorised KJV Bible"},{"year":"1859","event":"Darwin's On the Origin of Species explicitly references Ussher's timescale as the framework being overturned"}],"places":[{"name":"Dublin, Ireland","role":"Publication city"},{"name":"Armagh, Northern Ireland","role":"Episcopal See of Author"},{"name":"London, England","role":"Center of scholarly dissemination"}],"quote":"","externalLinks":[],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_THOUGHT","INTELLECTUAL_HISTORY","PRINT_CULTURE_AND_KNOWLEDGE","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"sessionId":"vscode-batch-67-may2026","enrichedBy":"claude-sonnet-4.6·cloud·GH#vscode","enrichedAt":"2026-05-17T04:19:49.668663+00:00","_editLog":[],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The *Annales Veteris Testamenti* established a widely accepted biblical chronology that influenced Western historical thought and scientific inquiry for over two centuries, becoming a touchstone for creationist views.","significanceCategory":"regional"}}
